# C# Concepts

## 1. Introduction to C#

C# is a modern, object-oriented, strongly typed programming language developed by Microsoft. It is mainly used with the .NET platform to build console, web, desktop, cloud, mobile and game applications.

```csharp
using System;

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Hello, C#!");
    }
}
```

## 2. Variables and Data Types

A variable is a named storage location used to hold a value.

| Data type | Purpose | Example |
|---|---|---|
| `int` | Integer number | `10` |
| `double` | Decimal number | `10.5` |
| `float` | Small decimal number | `10.5f` |
| `decimal` | High-precision decimal | `100.50m` |
| `char` | One character | `'A'` |
| `bool` | Boolean value | `true` or `false` |
| `string` | Text | `"Hello"` |
| `var` | Implicitly typed local variable | `var name = "Riya";` |

```csharp
int age = 20;
double percentage = 85.5;
string name = "Riya";
bool isPassed = true;
```

### Value Types and Reference Types

- **Value types** store the actual value. Example: `int`, `double`, `bool`, `struct`.
- **Reference types** store a reference to the object. Example: `string`, `class`, `array`, `interface`.

## 3. Input and Output

```csharp
Console.Write("Enter your name: ");
string name = Console.ReadLine();
Console.WriteLine("Hello, " + name);
```

- `Console.Write()` prints text without a new line.
- `Console.WriteLine()` prints text and moves to the next line.
- `Console.ReadLine()` reads a string from the user.

## 4. Operators

### Arithmetic Operators

`+`, `-`, `*`, `/`, `%`

### Relational Operators

`==`, `!=`, `>`, `<`, `>=`, `<=`

### Logical Operators

`&&` (AND), `||` (OR), `!` (NOT)

### Assignment Operators

`=`, `+=`, `-=`, `*=`, `/=`, `%=`

## 5. Conditional Statements

### `if` Statement

```csharp
if (marks >= 40)
{
    Console.WriteLine("Pass");}
else
{
    Console.WriteLine("Fail");
}
```

### `switch` Statement

```csharp
switch (day)
{
    case 1:
        Console.WriteLine("Monday");
        break;
    default:
        Console.WriteLine("Invalid day");
        break;
}
```

## 6. Loops

### `for` Loop

```csharp
for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++)
{
    Console.WriteLine(i);
}
```

### `while` Loop

```csharp
int i = 1;
while (i <= 5)
{
    Console.WriteLine(i);
    i++;
}
```

### `do-while` Loop

```csharp
int i = 1;
do
{
    Console.WriteLine(i);
    i++;
}
while (i <= 5);
```

### `foreach` Loop

```csharp
int[] numbers = { 10, 20, 30 };

foreach (int number in numbers)
{
    Console.WriteLine(number);
}
```

## 7. Arrays

An array stores multiple values of the same data type.

```csharp
int[] marks = new int[3];
marks[0] = 70;
marks[1] = 80;
marks[2] = 90;

string[] names = { "Riya", "Raj", "Mita" };
```

Array indexes start from zero. The first element is at index `0`.

## 8. Methods

A method is a named block of code that performs a specific task.

```csharp
static int Add(int a, int b)
{
    return a + b;
}

int result = Add(10, 20);
```

### Method Components

- `static` means the method belongs to the class.
- The return type is the type of value returned by the method.
- `void` means no value is returned.
- Parameters receive input values.
- `return` sends a value back to the caller.

## 9. Classes and Objects

A class is a blueprint used to define the properties and behavior of objects.

```csharp
class Student
{
    public int RollNumber;
    public string Name;

    public void Display()
    {
        Console.WriteLine(RollNumber + " " + Name);
    }
}
```

An object is an instance of a class.

```csharp
Student student = new Student();
student.RollNumber = 101;
student.Name = "Riya";
student.Display();
```

## 10. Constructors

A constructor initializes an object when it is created.

```csharp
class Student
{
    public int RollNumber;

    public Student(int rollNumber)
    {
        RollNumber = rollNumber;
    }
}

Student student = new Student(101);
```

### Constructor Characteristics

- It has the same name as the class.
- It does not have a return type.
- It is called automatically with `new`.
- It can be overloaded.

## 11. Properties

Properties control access to private fields.

```csharp
class Person
{
    private string name;

    public string Name
    {
        get { return name; }
        set { name = value; }
    }
}
```

## 12. Access Modifiers

| Modifier | Accessible from |
|---|---|
| `public` | Anywhere |
| `private` | Same class |
| `protected` | Same class and derived classes |
| `internal` | Same assembly |
| `protected internal` | Same assembly or derived class |
| `private protected` | Derived classes in the same assembly |

## 13. Static Members

Static members belong to the class rather than a specific object.

```csharp
class Counter
{
    public static int Count;
}

Counter.Count++;
```

## 14. `this` Keyword

`this` refers to the current instance of a class.

```csharp
class Student
{
    private int rollNumber;

    public Student(int rollNumber)
    {
        this.rollNumber = rollNumber;
    }
}
```

## 15. Inheritance

Inheritance allows a class to reuse members from another class.

```csharp
class Animal
{
    public void Eat()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Eating");
    }
}

class Dog : Animal
{
    public void Bark()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Barking");
    }
}
```

- Base class: `Animal`
- Derived class: `Dog`
- C# supports single inheritance for classes.

## 16. Polymorphism

Polymorphism allows the same interface to have different implementations.

### Method Overloading

Two or more methods in the same class have the same name but different parameters.

```csharp
int Add(int a, int b) => a + b;
double Add(double a, double b) => a + b;
```

### Method Overriding

A derived class provides a new implementation of a base-class method.

```csharp
class Animal
{
    public virtual void Sound()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Some sound");
    }
}

class Cat : Animal
{
    public override void Sound()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Meow");
    }
}
```

## 17. Abstraction

Abstraction exposes essential features while hiding implementation details.

### Abstract Class

```csharp
abstract class Shape
{
    public abstract double Area();
}

class Circle : Shape
{
    public double Radius;

    public override double Area()
    {
        return Math.PI * Radius * Radius;
    }
}
```

### Interface

An interface defines a contract that a class must implement.

```csharp
interface IAnimal
{
    void MakeSound();
}

class Cat : IAnimal
{
    public void MakeSound()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Meow");
    }
}
```

## 18. Encapsulation

Encapsulation means grouping data and methods in a class and restricting direct access to important data using access modifiers and properties.

```csharp
class BankAccount
{
    private double balance;

    public void Deposit(double amount)
    {
        if (amount > 0)
            balance += amount;
    }

    public double GetBalance()
    {
        return balance;
    }
}
```

## 19. Exception Handling

Exceptions represent errors that occur during program execution.

```csharp
try
{
    int number = Convert.ToInt32(Console.ReadLine());
    Console.WriteLine(100 / number);
}
catch (DivideByZeroException)
{
    Console.WriteLine("Cannot divide by zero.");
}
catch (FormatException)
{
    Console.WriteLine("Please enter a valid number.");
}
finally
{
    Console.WriteLine("Program finished.");
}
```

## 20. Collections

Collections are used to store and manage groups of objects.

```csharp
using System.Collections.Generic;

List<int> numbers = new List<int>();
numbers.Add(10);
numbers.Add(20);

foreach (int number in numbers)
{
    Console.WriteLine(number);
}
```

Common collection types include:

- `List<T>`
- `Dictionary<TKey, TValue>`
- `Queue<T>`
- `Stack<T>`
- `HashSet<T>`

## 21. LINQ

LINQ is used to query collections, arrays and databases using C# syntax.

```csharp
int[] numbers = { 10, 25, 30, 45, 60 };

var evenNumbers = from number in numbers
                  where number % 2 == 0
                  select number;

foreach (int number in evenNumbers)
{
    Console.WriteLine(number);
}
```

## 22. Common C# Keywords

| Keyword | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `class` | Declares a class |
| `new` | Creates an object or array |
| `this` | Refers to the current object |
| `base` | Refers to the base class |
| `static` | Declares class-level members |
| `readonly` | Makes a field assignable only during declaration or in a constructor |
| `const` | Declares a compile-time constant |
| `null` | Represents no object reference |
| `is` | Tests a type |
| `as` | Attempts a type conversion without throwing an exception |
| `sealed` | Prevents a class from being inherited or a virtual method from being overridden further |

## 23. Important Viva Questions and Answers

### Q1. What is C#?

C# is a modern, strongly typed, object-oriented programming language used with the .NET platform.

### Q2. What is a variable?

A variable is a named memory location used to store data.

### Q3. What is the difference between `int` and `string`?

- `int` stores whole numbers.
- `string` stores text.

### Q4. What is a data type?

A data type defines the kind of value a variable can store and the operations that can be performed on it.

### Q5. What is the difference between `Console.Write()` and `Console.WriteLine()`?

- `Console.Write()` prints without starting a new line.
- `Console.WriteLine()` prints and moves to the next line.

### Q6. What is a class?

A class is a blueprint or template that defines fields, properties and methods.

### Q7. What is an object?

An object is an instance of a class.

### Q8. What is the use of the `new` keyword?

The `new` keyword creates a new object or array.

### Q9. What is a constructor?

A constructor is a special method that initializes an object. It has the same name as the class and no return type.

### Q10. What is a method?

A method is a block of code that performs a specific task.

### Q11. What is the difference between method overloading and overriding?

- Overloading has the same method name with different parameters in the same class.
- Overriding provides a new implementation for an inherited virtual or abstract method.

### Q12. What is inheritance?

Inheritance allows a derived class to reuse fields and methods from a base class.

### Q13. What is encapsulation?

Encapsulation is the process of wrapping data and methods together and controlling access to the data.

### Q14. What is abstraction?

Abstraction hides implementation details and exposes only essential functionality.

### Q15. What is polymorphism?

Polymorphism means that one interface or operation can have multiple implementations.

### Q16. What is an interface?

An interface is a contract containing method and property declarations that implementing classes must provide.

### Q17. What is the difference between an abstract class and an interface?

- An abstract class can contain abstract and implemented members and fields.
- An interface defines a contract and does not normally provide instance fields.
- A class can inherit only one class but can implement multiple interfaces.

### Q18. What is an exception?

An exception is an unexpected runtime problem that disrupts normal program execution.

### Q19. What is the purpose of `try-catch-finally`?

- `try` contains code that may raise an exception.
- `catch` handles an exception.
- `finally` contains code that runs whether or not an exception occurs.

### Q20. What is the difference between a list and an array?

- An array has a fixed size.
- A `List<T>` can grow or shrink dynamically.

### Q21. What is LINQ?

LINQ stands for Language-Integrated Query. It provides a consistent syntax for querying collections and other data sources.

### Q22. What is the default accessibility of class members?

Class members are private by default if no access modifier is specified.

### Q23. What is the difference between `value type` and `reference type`?

- A value type stores its value directly.
- A reference type stores a reference to the object's location.

### Q24. What is a static member?

A static member belongs to the class and is shared by all objects of the class.

### Q25. What is namespace in C#?

A namespace organizes related classes and prevents naming conflicts.
